{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":42751,"duration_api_ms":42751,"is_error":false,"result":"Found it. In `repo/search.py`, `insert_index` does a leftmost bisect: when `sorted_list[mid] >= x` it moves `hi` down, so equal elements push the insertion point left of the existing duplicates. For a sorted index that wants equal keys to land *after* the existing ones, it needs to be a rightmost bisect.Tests pass. \n\nThe fix in `repo/search.py`: the comparison was `sorted_list[mid] < x` with `else: hi = mid`, which is a leftmost bisect — duplicates of `x` cause `hi` to collapse leftward, so the returned index points *before* the existing equal keys. Changing the condition to `sorted_list[mid] <= x` (advancing `lo` past equal elements) makes it a rightmost bisect, so equal keys insert *after* the existing ones and stay grouped in the sorted index.","session_id":"caa82540-9858-438c-902f-21a290301033","request_id":"7d58973e-f05b-45c8-ae3d-2cd47a8fec25","usage":{"inputTokens":9640,"outputTokens":1293,"cacheReadTokens":86016,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
